{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/sarif-2.1/schema/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "fullName": "Trivy Vulnerability Scanner",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy",
          "name": "Trivy",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "GHSA-g98v-hv3f-hcfr",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "atty potential unaligned read"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "On windows, `atty` dereferences a potentially unaligned pointer.\n\nIn practice however, the pointer won't be unaligned unless a custom global allocator is used.\n\nIn particular, the `System` allocator on windows uses `HeapAlloc`, which guarantees a large enough alignment.\n\n# atty is Unmaintained\n\nA Pull Request with a fix has been provided over a year ago but the maintainer seems to be unreachable.\n\nLast release of `atty` was almost 3 years ago.\n\n## Possible Alternative(s)\n\nThe below list has not been vetted in any way and may or may not contain alternatives;\n\n- [std::io::IsTerminal](https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/io/trait.IsTerminal.html) - Stable since Rust 1.70.0\n- [is-terminal](https://crates.io/crates/is-terminal) - Standalone crate supporting Rust older than 1.70.0\"\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-g98v-hv3f-hcfr",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability GHSA-g98v-hv3f-hcfr\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: atty\nFixed Version: \nLink: [GHSA-g98v-hv3f-hcfr](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-g98v-hv3f-hcfr)\nOn windows, `atty` dereferences a potentially unaligned pointer.\n\nIn practice however, the pointer won't be unaligned unless a custom global allocator is used.\n\nIn particular, the `System` allocator on windows uses `HeapAlloc`, which guarantees a large enough alignment.\n\n# atty is Unmaintained\n\nA Pull Request with a fix has been provided over a year ago but the maintainer seems to be unreachable.\n\nLast release of `atty` was almost 3 years ago.\n\n## Possible Alternative(s)\n\nThe below list has not been vetted in any way and may or may not contain alternatives;\n\n- [std::io::IsTerminal](https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/io/trait.IsTerminal.html) - Stable since Rust 1.70.0\n- [is-terminal](https://crates.io/crates/is-terminal) - Standalone crate supporting Rust older than 1.70.0\"\n",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability GHSA-g98v-hv3f-hcfr**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|atty||[GHSA-g98v-hv3f-hcfr](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-g98v-hv3f-hcfr)|\n\nOn windows, `atty` dereferences a potentially unaligned pointer.\n\nIn practice however, the pointer won't be unaligned unless a custom global allocator is used.\n\nIn particular, the `System` allocator on windows uses `HeapAlloc`, which guarantees a large enough alignment.\n\n# atty is Unmaintained\n\nA Pull Request with a fix has been provided over a year ago but the maintainer seems to be unreachable.\n\nLast release of `atty` was almost 3 years ago.\n\n## Possible Alternative(s)\n\nThe below list has not been vetted in any way and may or may not contain alternatives;\n\n- [std::io::IsTerminal](https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/io/trait.IsTerminal.html) - Stable since Rust 1.70.0\n- [is-terminal](https://crates.io/crates/is-terminal) - Standalone crate supporting Rust older than 1.70.0\"\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2022-23639",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "crossbeam-utils provides atomics, synchronization primitives, scoped t ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "crossbeam-utils provides atomics, synchronization primitives, scoped threads, and other utilities for concurrent programming in Rust. crossbeam-utils prior to version 0.8.7 incorrectly assumed that the alignment of `{i,u}64` was always the same as `Atomic{I,U}64`. However, the alignment of `{i,u}64` on a 32-bit target can be smaller than `Atomic{I,U}64`. This can cause unaligned memory accesses and data race. Crates using `fetch_*` methods with `AtomicCell\u003c{i,u}64\u003e` are affected by this issue. 32-bit targets without `Atomic{I,U}64` and 64-bit targets are not affected by this issue. This has been fixed in crossbeam-utils 0.8.7. There are currently no known workarounds."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2022-23639",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2022-23639\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: crossbeam-utils\nFixed Version: 0.8.7\nLink: [CVE-2022-23639](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2022-23639)\ncrossbeam-utils provides atomics, synchronization primitives, scoped threads, and other utilities for concurrent programming in Rust. crossbeam-utils prior to version 0.8.7 incorrectly assumed that the alignment of `{i,u}64` was always the same as `Atomic{I,U}64`. However, the alignment of `{i,u}64` on a 32-bit target can be smaller than `Atomic{I,U}64`. This can cause unaligned memory accesses and data race. Crates using `fetch_*` methods with `AtomicCell\u003c{i,u}64\u003e` are affected by this issue. 32-bit targets without `Atomic{I,U}64` and 64-bit targets are not affected by this issue. This has been fixed in crossbeam-utils 0.8.7. There are currently no known workarounds.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2022-23639**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|crossbeam-utils|0.8.7|[CVE-2022-23639](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2022-23639)|\n\ncrossbeam-utils provides atomics, synchronization primitives, scoped threads, and other utilities for concurrent programming in Rust. crossbeam-utils prior to version 0.8.7 incorrectly assumed that the alignment of `{i,u}64` was always the same as `Atomic{I,U}64`. However, the alignment of `{i,u}64` on a 32-bit target can be smaller than `Atomic{I,U}64`. This can cause unaligned memory accesses and data race. Crates using `fetch_*` methods with `AtomicCell\u003c{i,u}64\u003e` are affected by this issue. 32-bit targets without `Atomic{I,U}64` and 64-bit targets are not affected by this issue. This has been fixed in crossbeam-utils 0.8.7. There are currently no known workarounds."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 8.1,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "8.1",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GHSA-9hpw-r23r-xgm5",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Data race in `Iter` and `IterMut`"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the affected version of this crate, `{Iter, IterMut}::next` used a weaker memory ordering when loading values than what was required, exposing a potential data race\nwhen iterating over a `ThreadLocal`'s values.\n\nCrates using `Iter::next`, or `IterMut::next` are affected by this issue.\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9hpw-r23r-xgm5",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability GHSA-9hpw-r23r-xgm5\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: thread_local\nFixed Version: 1.1.4\nLink: [GHSA-9hpw-r23r-xgm5](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9hpw-r23r-xgm5)\nIn the affected version of this crate, `{Iter, IterMut}::next` used a weaker memory ordering when loading values than what was required, exposing a potential data race\nwhen iterating over a `ThreadLocal`'s values.\n\nCrates using `Iter::next`, or `IterMut::next` are affected by this issue.\n",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability GHSA-9hpw-r23r-xgm5**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|thread_local|1.1.4|[GHSA-9hpw-r23r-xgm5](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9hpw-r23r-xgm5)|\n\nIn the affected version of this crate, `{Iter, IterMut}::next` used a weaker memory ordering when loading values than what was required, exposing a potential data race\nwhen iterating over a `ThreadLocal`'s values.\n\nCrates using `Iter::next`, or `IterMut::next` are affected by this issue.\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "version": "0.71.2"
        }
      },
